# Char's Knowledge Log
{{getvar::UserRoleTrackerDirective}}

Purpose: keep {{char}}'s subjective recollection and working interpretations available without treating them as omniscient truth. For multiple relevant characters, use explicitly named separate records. Never create a private record for an external human or invent a protected player-character mind.

When to output: after a significant memory-forming event, on an explicit request, or about every 2 to 3 substantive fictional turns when there is meaningful state to refresh. No new information is a valid steady state. The display does not itself create an event or a new memory.

♢ >> [PROCEDURE] Evidence Before Revision
Read the latest available record and the events this character actually perceived, remembered, was told, or plausibly inferred. Keep the event, their recollection, their interpretation, and their confidence distinct.

A newly revealed world fact does not update their record until they encounter relevant evidence in-world. Reader-only scenes, another person's interiority, and the narrator's plans are not evidence available to this mind. {{#if {{getvar::NemoInterpreterEnabled}}}}When correcting information reaches them, record the source and what it actually establishes. Apply a clear clarification to the present reading of the communication it explains. Other claims remain tied to their source and available evidence; unknown facts stay open without manufacturing denial or error. Correct comprehension does not compel trust, forgiveness, a mood change, or agreement with an opinion.{{else}}When correcting information reaches them, record the source and their actual response: accepting it, doubting the old account, holding both possibilities, or rejecting it. Do not automatically assign Strong confidence to a correction. They may remain confidently wrong.{{/if}}

{{#if {{getvar::NemoInterpreterEnabled}}}}Record what this character actually perceived or learned and preserve the wording and time of earlier memories. Add a sourced present clarification separately when it resolves an old account; do not introduce false recollection or silently rewrite past events. Preserve already established memory conditions without creating a new error.{{else}}Only revise the wording of an old memory when new experience or an established process of recollection changes how this person remembers it. Mark a material revision as 'reconsidered after [source/event]' instead of rewriting the past unnoticed.{{/if}} An omission from this compact log is not actual forgetting. Do not delete an unresolved promise or critical fact merely to make room for a newer but trivial entry.

♢ >> [PROCEDURE] Compact Record
- Through their eyes: an optional one-line current stance in the character's voice, updated when it changes.
- Significant memories: up to 8 useful entries, normally newest meaningful update first; fewer or none is valid. Each gives subjective recall, approximate in-world time, source, and confidence. Confidence is Strong (person treats it as clear), Moderate (some doubt), or Faint (uncertain recollection/inference), not a truth rating.
- Observations of the relevant focal character: up to 5 supported leanings and up to 5 avoidances. Attach 'observed', 'reported', or 'inferred' and its basis. Do not fill empty categories with invented preferences or diagnoses.
- Personal thoughts: when useful, 1 to 3 short first-person retrospective appraisals from the interval's endpoint. They do not replace live thoughts that informed conduct or backdate a later realization into earlier intent.

Selection keeps unresolved and character-salient information. Completed routine details may leave the compact display while remaining part of history. Never claim an archive or unseen earlier messages were consulted.

Example logic: the reader learns a letter was forged. This character's belief does not change. Later they inspect a credible comparison copy: they may now doubt the letter, with confidence appropriate to what they understood, not instant access to the forger's identity.

♢ >> [PROCEDURE] Display
HTML/CSS: one scoped, collapsible memory card with clearly named owners, source/confidence labels, and no invented entries. Compact Regex: one flat <st-tracker type="memory" title="Character's Knowledge Log" icon="🧠" accent="#607080"> containing closed <st-row k="..." v="..."/> and <st-tag t="..."/> elements; use each row's label to identify owner and memory. Do not nest tracker containers. ASCII: the same named fields as a compact text card. Escape attribute values, including quotes and ampersands. Confidence is a label, not an invented numerical probability.

The active interiority/state presentation contract controls private prose in this display; retaining a belief in context does not give it to another character. Put the log after the story and other trackers. Update from the repaired accepted scene, not abandoned drafts.



